1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
that makes sense, because you're losing this on every unit.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:08,040
Every unit that gets produced by the incompetent firm has waste.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:10,800
So if the firm that is in the market

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:15,480
is producing at 10% above the minimum social minimum average

5
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:19,280
cost, then every unit has its 10% waste.

6
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:21,280
And that is a problem.

7
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,360
So again, if you're curious about, well,

8
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:26,480
I guess these estimates are like 30 years old from the Robert

9
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:27,680
Borg's Antitrust Paradox.

10
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:29,800
Although I don't think there have been many efforts

11
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,260
to make further estimates of this,

12
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:33,760
people just lost interest in the subject.

13
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:34,640
Same with me.

14
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,400
Why?

15
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,120
There's an element of fashion in academia.

16
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:40,320
I don't know.

17
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:42,080
This may come as a terrible shock.

18
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,360
But sometimes people aren't interested in a topic

19
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:46,360
because nobody else is doing it, even though it's

20
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:47,240
really important.

21
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:48,680
And sometimes people devote their lives

22
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:50,640
to topics that are of no importance whatsoever,

23
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:53,360
because other people are doing it.

24
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,480
You can try to either game the system or approve it.

25
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,840
That will be up to you.

26
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,720
All right.

27
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:01,220
Yes?

28
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:02,960
Why do you draw your marginal cost curves

29
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:04,600
horizontally instead of vertically?

30
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,000
So the answer is that it's easier.

31
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,280
And it's not wrong.

32
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:09,760
It's just a special case.

33
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:13,320
But yes, we could go and do these as,

34
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:14,880
we could go and draw them like that.

35
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:17,160
And then the diagram just gets harder.

36
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:20,040
But everything I was saying still goes through.

37
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:23,160
There is a common question about this, which is,

38
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,280
why don't we go and say that that's the allocated,

39
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:27,720
that's the productive inefficiency,

40
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,120
and then call that the new allocated inefficiency

41
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:31,480
or something like that?

42
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:33,320
And the answer is, we could totally do that.

43
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:34,560
But it's confusing.

44
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:39,000
So it is less confusing to say that everything that

45
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,280
is caused by not letting the lowest cost producer produce,

46
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:44,720
we'll call all that productive inefficiency.

47
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:48,880
And then we'll only call allocated inefficiency

48
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,640
the deadweight costs that come from your price being greater

49
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:53,880
than your own marginal cost.

50
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,840
So now, going back to the scenario,

51
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:59,960
imagine that after you go and put the entrant out

52
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:03,000
of business, you say, I also want to make some money here.

53
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,800
In that case, you're going to go and set the price that

54
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:11,040
is above not only the minimum marginal cost,

55
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:14,000
but you're going to set it above your own marginal cost.

56
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,320
So if you are a high-cost firm and you want to make money,

57
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:22,320
you need to charge a price higher than your own high cost.

58
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:25,080
And in fact, you will go and set marginal revenue

59
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,520
equal to the incumbent marginal cost, this high one.

60
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,960
And then we will have two kinds of inefficiency.

61
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:37,560
We'll have a trapezoid of productive inefficiency.

62
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,200
And then we'll also have the triangle

63
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:43,800
of allocated inefficiency.

64
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:46,600
Is it overlap?

65
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:47,240
No, actually.

66
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,040
It's designed to not have overlap.

67
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:51,840
So we only call the price being greater

68
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,160
than your own marginal cost, the allocated.

69
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:57,880
And then we call everything else the productive.

70
00:02:57,880 --> 00:02:58,920
Could you relabel it?

71
00:02:58,920 --> 00:02:59,560
Yes, you could.

72
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:00,720
But it's confusing.

73
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:01,680
It's more confusing.

74
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,080
It is clearer if you do it this way.

75
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:06,560
So what's determining that point?

76
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:07,200
This one here?

77
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:07,700
Yeah.

78
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,440
Oh, well, so that's just the standard equation

79
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,400
for the monopolist, where you put in your own,

80
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:13,960
the monopolist's own marginal cost.

81
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:15,240
So the marginal revenue curve.

82
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,640
Yeah, so the marginal revenue curve

83
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:19,920
is the same either way, because there's just one demand curve.

84
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:22,600
But there are two different monopoly prices

85
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,440
that you'll get, depending upon whether you

86
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:27,720
have an efficient monopolist or an inefficient monopolist.

87
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,800
So yes, an important point for understanding

88
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,040
the basic theory of monopoly.

89
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,200
So let's take the worst monopoly in the world.

90
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:37,600
Does standard monopoly theory predict that the,

91
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,880
what does standard monopoly theory predict they will do

92
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:42,600
if their own marginal costs fall?

93
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,600
So this is like the really crummy monopolist,

94
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:47,280
where it's illegal for anyone to compete with them.

95
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:50,200
And then, say, the cost of oil goes down.

96
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:53,440
What does standard monopoly theory predict they will do?

97
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:54,920
Lower price.

98
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:55,600
Yes.

99
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,600
Why would they lower price on their monopoly?

100
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,800
Because they want to go and set a price equal to,

101
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,400
marginal revenue equals marginal cost,

102
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,000
is what maximizes a monopolist's profit.

103
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,120
So if marginal cost goes down, even the worst monopolist

104
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:11,280
in the world wants to cut his price.

105
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,200
As long as it's profit.

106
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:14,040
As long as it's profit maximizing.

107
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:15,760
If it were a regulated monopoly that's

108
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:17,280
not allowed to earn profits, then it

109
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:20,960
would not decide to do it.

110
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:21,960
All right.

111
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:25,440
So now, I'm just thinking about examples of this.

112
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:27,960
My favorite one of a regulated monopolist

113
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:32,360
that doesn't allow competition is the post office.

114
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:34,920
So as you probably know, it is illegal for anyone

115
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:36,760
to compete with the United States Post Office

116
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:39,600
for delivery of first class mail.

117
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,120
So the post office first bans competition.

118
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:45,040
But then, it has its prices regulated.

119
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,720
So it is not allowed to earn really high profits.

120
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:49,880
And this makes for great public relations.

121
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:52,800
Because you can always say, all of these ignorant critics

122
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:55,000
of the post office who act like they're making money, hand

123
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:55,560
them a fist.

124
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:56,200
We're not.

125
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:59,240
We're just barely scraping by here.

126
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:01,440
And the point of this is a, that's true,

127
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:04,760
and yet you're still screwing us over.

128
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:08,520
Because you are depriving us of alternatives that are better.

129
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:12,520
All right.

130
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:13,020
All right.

131
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:19,320
Now, this also gives us some great insight

132
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:20,840
into government created monopolies

133
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,400
versus private monopoly versus market monopolies.

134
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,000
So both of them have potential for allocating inefficiency.

135
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,200
So remember, we go back over here.

136
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,680
So here, if there's a whole distribution

137
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:33,800
of different costs, and the firm that

138
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:37,840
happens to be the lowest cost will be the monopolist.

139
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,200
And what price will they charge?

140
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:42,320
You're already established?

141
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,200
So what price will the incumbent firm charge

142
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,080
in this simple contestability model?

143
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:46,720
Marginal cost.

144
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:49,920
Yeah, just below the marginal cost of the potential firm.

145
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:52,560
Suppose there's 100 potential entrants.

146
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:54,560
Do you take an average, or what do you do?

147
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:55,920
Go by whatever the lowest is.

148
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,920
Yes, the second best.

149
00:05:57,920 --> 00:05:58,480
Second best.

150
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:02,520
Intuitively, what do you need to do to win a running race?

151
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:05,680
You need to run faster than the second fastest person.

152
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:06,840
Then you win.

153
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,680
So similarly, to be the monopolist,

154
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:15,160
you need to have lower cost than the second lowest cost firm.

155
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,200
So anyway, we take a look over here.

156
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:19,760
So we charge that price.

157
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:22,280
So is there going to be any allocated inefficiency

158
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:24,320
in this model?

159
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:24,840
Well, sure.

160
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:25,920
Here's your marginal cost.

161
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,240
There's your price.

162
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,160
So there's allocated inefficiency.

163
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:31,760
Because you've got price greater than your own marginal cost,

164
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:33,640
so you do have that allocated inefficiency.

165
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:36,120
But is there any productive inefficiency?

166
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:39,280
No, because in this model, the productively inefficient firms

167
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:42,200
are out-competed and die, or they simply never

168
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:43,680
form in the first place.

169
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:47,080
And the firm that stays around is the lowest cost firm.

170
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:52,560
So this says that with money monopoly,

171
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:54,680
you may very well get allocated inefficiency.

172
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,880
In fact, you should expect allocated inefficiency.

173
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:01,760
Unless the two best firms in the world

174
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:05,160
are tied for awesomeness.

175
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:07,840
If the two best firms in the world have the very same cost,

176
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:10,320
then you get allocated inefficiency.

177
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:13,040
Otherwise, you always do.

178
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:16,800
But at least then you don't get productive inefficiency.

179
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,760
On the other hand, when you have a government-created monopoly,

180
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:22,200
then if the prices are not regulated,

181
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:25,280
should you still expect allocated inefficiency?

182
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:27,840
So you can charge any price that you want,

183
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:30,280
and the government makes competition illegal.

184
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:33,440
Will you raise price above your own marginal cost?

185
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,120
That make sense?

186
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:38,080
But is there likely to be productive inefficiency

187
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,120
in this scenario?

188
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,080
In fact, you should expect there to almost always

189
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:44,840
be productive inefficiency.

190
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,080
There's only one way that productive inefficiency

191
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:48,440
will be avoided.

192
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:51,680
That's if the government hands the monopoly to?

193
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:52,440
Markets.

194
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:55,640
Yes, the lowest cost firm in the world.

195
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:59,200
And why would it do that?

196
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:02,560
That seems like wishful thinking.

197
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:06,320
So it just so happens to be that all the dictator's brother-in-laws

198
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:09,400
are the best at producing stuff.

199
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:11,080
Why else would I make my brother-in-law,

200
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:12,880
unless he was a really low-cost producer?

201
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:20,760
So the idea there is that the very fact that you

202
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:22,360
are going to the government and crying

203
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,040
about needing production from competition

204
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:25,840
suggests that you probably aren't that good.

205
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,800
And that aside, there's just the fact, well,

206
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:33,120
what are the odds that the government will pick the firm

207
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:34,800
that has the very lowest costs?

208
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:37,320
Unless there's a mechanism that ensures the government always

209
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:41,680
picks them, then there is going to be a very high probability

210
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:43,800
that somebody else gets picked.

211
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,720
Furthermore, in the real dynamic world,

212
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:51,040
suppose the government initially picks the most efficient firm.

213
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:53,520
Is productive inefficiency likely to emerge later

214
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:57,280
on in a dynamic world?

215
00:08:57,280 --> 00:08:57,800
Sure.

216
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:58,300
Why?

217
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,600
Yeah.

218
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:02,120
New firms with lower costs.

219
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:04,520
Yes, new firms with lower costs are born.

220
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:06,160
And the government doesn't immediately

221
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:07,480
grab the license of the monopolist

222
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:08,880
and hand it to the new firm.

223
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,920
Instead, they stand by their firm.

224
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:15,440
They say, well, this firm represents France itself.

225
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:18,880
So no, we can't just go and give the license away

226
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:21,160
to some other firm, who supposedly

227
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:24,040
has lower costs and everything.

228
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:25,800
People act like just because it's illegal

229
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:28,520
that we're scared of something, which we're totally not.

230
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:31,600
So that is the idea.

231
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:34,320
So the key difference between government-created monopolies

232
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:36,720
and market monopolies, both of them

233
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:39,080
allow for allocated inefficiency.

234
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:41,280
But government-created monopolies

235
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:44,000
are much more likely to have productive inefficiency, too,

236
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,760
which, as we said, in the real world seems to be much worse.

237
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:53,280
So why don't we go and take our 10-minute break now.

238
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:54,560
And let's see.

239
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:58,640
So yes, you've got homework due today, correct?

240
00:09:58,640 --> 00:09:59,840
Just due today?

241
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:00,340
Yes.

242
00:10:00,340 --> 00:10:00,840
Yes.

243
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,640
So there's a look of non-recognition.

244
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:06,520
And when you go and, yes, and when you get it,

245
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:08,800
then please pick up the answer key.

246
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:11,360
And also, let's see.

247
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:17,400
And yes, also, never stops.

248
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:21,320
The hunger for econ cannot be quenched.

249
00:10:21,320 --> 00:10:22,960
OK.

250
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:23,480
All right.

251
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:25,520
So moving on.

252
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:27,560
All right, so predation, end-returns, and mixed

253
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:28,920
strategies.

254
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:32,080
Now, predation is one of those very vague words

255
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:34,440
that gets thrown around a lot.

256
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:37,640
So what insight can mean theory shed

257
00:10:37,640 --> 00:10:41,040
on what predation ought to mean, or the way

258
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:42,520
that we might interpret predation?

259
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,680
Actually, could you please close the door back there?

260
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:49,120
Before I do this, I have to tell you

261
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:50,960
one other thing about sunk costs.

262
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:53,160
So you know the standard story about once you really

263
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,920
understand economics, you walk out of movies freely,

264
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:58,960
because you realize you've already made it at some cost.

265
00:10:58,960 --> 00:11:02,880
And so why sit there enjoying, or why sit there not enjoying

266
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,760
a movie, when just because you wasted some money on it?

267
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:08,360
By then, you're wasting doubly.

268
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:12,800
Then the last time that I was in California, as I often do,

269
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:15,720
I learned something new and strange about my brother.

270
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:20,280
My brother is so cheap that he actually

271
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:23,680
asks for his money back for any movie he doesn't like.

272
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:29,480
Which means that for him, even though it is not a sunk cost.

273
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:30,880
Now, think about this.

274
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:33,160
So should this make you more or less likely to walk out

275
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:34,840
of a movie if when you walk out, you

276
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:38,080
feel like you also feel entitled to get your money back?

277
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:39,600
Or, of course, is that what you want?

278
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:40,960
Yes.

279
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:46,600
So the fact that if a cost is sunk,

280
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:48,640
it should make you more inclined to stay.

281
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:51,400
So you might say, I paid $7 for this.

282
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:53,160
I only like it at $2, but it sunk,

283
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:54,680
so I guess I'll spend the last $2,

284
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:56,280
and I'll get $2 surplus out of it.

285
00:11:56,280 --> 00:11:57,960
But yeah, if you get the whole $7 back,

286
00:11:57,960 --> 00:11:59,160
you'll walk out right away.

287
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:00,360
Apparently, this works, too.

288
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:01,680
I mean, the people are confused.

289
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:05,200
But all right, strange, cheap, middle-aged man.

290
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:08,560
I'm going to give you a little bit of money.

291
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,800
Pardon me, please.

292
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,080
How often does it work?

293
00:12:12,080 --> 00:12:13,840
Apparently, whenever he has.

294
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:16,000
I think he even asks sometimes at the end of a movie

295
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:20,080
and feels that he was there because his family made

296
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:21,640
him stay there.

297
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:23,440
Well, I won't ask to bring it for my wife.

298
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:25,280
She liked it, but I want my money back.

299
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:26,200
And I have to suffer.

300
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:26,700
Yeah.

301
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:28,680
Or, all right.

302
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:29,640
Let's just go back.

303
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:33,320
So the fact, all right, yeah, yes?

304
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:36,040
I think it might be because some movie theaters have

305
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:39,680
an agreement that the ticket price goes exclusively

306
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:42,480
to the movie producers.

307
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:44,720
Well, it's not normally exclusively.

308
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:47,240
But yeah, it's true that at first it's usually at 90%,

309
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:49,000
and then the percentage goes down.

310
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:49,880
Yeah.

311
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:50,400
All right.

312
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:51,560
But they're still confused.

313
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:55,040
It's really unusual to do this for someone to ask.

314
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:56,240
All right.

315
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:58,760
So now, going back to predation.

316
00:12:58,760 --> 00:12:59,240
All right.

317
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:01,160
So the very simplest model of predation

318
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:04,960
is just that it is a hostile phrase that

319
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:06,800
means limit pricing.

320
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:09,480
All right, so imagine we've got a world just like this one.

321
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:12,040
We already drew it, where we've got lots of different cost

322
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:14,880
curves, marginal cost one, two, marginal cost three,

323
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:17,920
marginal cost four, marginal cost five, so on.

324
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:19,800
It's all the different costs.

325
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,600
And the most efficient firms charge the price

326
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:26,080
just low enough to keep everyone else out of the market.

327
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:26,920
All right.

328
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:32,840
So now, you can understand why you might call it predation,

329
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:34,560
because you charge the price low enough

330
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:37,760
to make it impossible for anyone to profitably compete with you.

331
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:40,600
It sounds kind of like you're being a predator, a wolf.

332
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:42,360
You're like, argh, all right, I'm

333
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:45,320
going to charge a price so none of you will be able to compete.

334
00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:49,120
I'm still profitable, but none of you are.

335
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:49,880
All right.

336
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:53,440
Now, in the real world, this probably happens all the time.

337
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:57,200
As to whether or not it is done self-consciously

338
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:58,840
is an interesting question.

339
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,000
So who really cares?

340
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,920
Well, the law cares a lot.

341
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,400
So if you are suing someone under the antitrust law,

342
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:07,560
and you've got to remember the way I'm going to show you,

343
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,560
so suppose you can either sue someone

344
00:14:10,560 --> 00:14:14,040
for breach of contract or for breaking the antitrust laws.

345
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:16,400
Which one do you definitely want to sue them under?

346
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:17,760
Breach of contract.

347
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:19,480
No, breach of contract.

348
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:22,360
And age old law says that if you prove that they breach

349
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:25,360
contract, they have to pay you their wages.

350
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:28,200
But if you can sue them under antitrust law,

351
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:30,640
then what do you get?

352
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:32,560
Treble damages.

353
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:33,520
Treble damages.

354
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:37,520
So I believe that's originally in the Clayton Act.

355
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,040
And this is why one of the most common kinds of private antitrust

356
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:45,560
cases is actually franchisees suing the mother corporation.

357
00:14:45,560 --> 00:14:47,680
Because if you sue the mother corporation for breach

358
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:49,560
of contract, you only get your money back.

359
00:14:49,560 --> 00:14:51,260
But if you can sell them under antitrust,

360
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:53,480
you get three times the whatever harm they did to you.

361
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:58,560
Now, so why are we talking about this?

362
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:03,600
Because in the eyes of the law, intent matters a lot.

363
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:05,520
Because remember, whenever you are actually

364
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:07,720
taking someone to trial, you need to convince 12 people not

365
00:15:07,720 --> 00:15:10,520
smart enough to get out of jury duty.

366
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:14,800
And therefore, what is likely to be very convincing to a jury

367
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:15,320
like this?

368
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:17,560
A bunch of diagrams and statistics and stuff?

369
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:18,360
No.

370
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:21,480
Far better is you want a one memo taken out

371
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:25,040
of the archives of the accused firm that says,

372
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:27,040
we will destroy the competition!

373
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:28,600
Exclamation point.

374
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:30,240
And then you wave it around and say,

375
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:32,040
we don't need to talk about bad statistics.

376
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:34,520
We have it in there from their own mouths.

377
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:37,120
And that is where your lawyer's like, oh, god.

378
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,320
Let's settle this now.

379
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:42,080
Because in the eyes of the law, as well as

380
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:44,640
the human beings that are involved in the law,

381
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:47,000
intentions are enormously important,

382
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,520
even though economically you might do this just because you

383
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:51,020
are civil.

384
00:15:51,020 --> 00:15:52,880
Like, we just chose the brightest and nicest

385
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:53,480
vice-profits.

386
00:15:53,480 --> 00:15:54,120
I don't know.

387
00:15:54,120 --> 00:15:55,720
I'm not trying to hurt anybody.

388
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:57,560
And then there might be another person,

389
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:01,280
say, like a former athlete, who's like, yeah, crush them!

390
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:01,960
Crush them!

391
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:04,080
Crack them in the ground!

392
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:07,920
And the behavior is identical in both cases.

393
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:09,760
But the attitude is different.

394
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:14,480
And if you put it in writing, then the legal outcome

395
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:16,120
is likely to be very different, too.

396
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:23,800
So you're saying this is illegal under antitrust

397
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:27,240
to intentionally undercut the prices of your company?

398
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:31,040
Well, so if you do it and you very clearly say,

399
00:16:31,040 --> 00:16:32,640
we are doing it in order to destroy,

400
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:35,760
in order to acquire a monopoly and destroy the competition,

401
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:39,360
then you are likely to be in big trouble.

402
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:41,080
Of course, as to what happens, again,

403
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:44,120
it always depends upon what the jury will find.

404
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:47,120
The jury is generally so economically ignorant,

405
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:49,320
I mean, to an almost unbelievable degree.

406
00:16:49,320 --> 00:16:50,720
And yet, they are making decisions

407
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:52,640
that could cost billions of dollars.

408
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:57,240
So of course, in most cases, they're a lot smaller than that.

409
00:16:57,240 --> 00:16:59,960
Yeah, so even like the biggest antitrust cases,

410
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:01,960
those are just regular old juries.

411
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:03,360
They don't need to go and even go

412
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,400
get a bachelor's degree in economics

413
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:07,960
to sit on the Microsoft case.

414
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:11,160
They sit there doing, well, I don't know a lot about this.

415
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:17,040
But what I do know is Microsoft is good.

416
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:19,560
And then they fail.

417
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:20,320
Or bad.

418
00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:21,120
And then they lose.

419
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:26,800
So this kind of thing probably happens all the time,

420
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,280
with or without predatory intent,

421
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,640
although the intent matters a lot if you're getting sued.

422
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:34,480
Now, a more interesting model than this,

423
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:37,200
this one is so basic that I'm going to admit

424
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:38,680
that it probably happens a lot.

425
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:40,840
But a more interesting model is something like this.

426
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:44,960
The incumbent has entry-dependent prices.

427
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,000
Where, so the incumbent has a policy, well,

428
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:47,720
here's what I do.

429
00:17:47,720 --> 00:17:49,400
I price high if there's no entrant,

430
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:50,800
and low if there's an entrant.

431
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:53,160
And then the entrant decides whether or not to enter.

432
00:17:53,160 --> 00:17:54,680
And of course, if you are the entrant

433
00:17:54,680 --> 00:17:57,680
and the incumbent has convinced you it's going to do this,

434
00:17:57,680 --> 00:17:59,200
again, you will look and you'll see

435
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:00,640
that he's making a lot of money right now.

436
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:01,800
He said, well, yeah, but I'm not going

437
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:03,440
to make a lot of money if I go in,

438
00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:05,440
because the incumbent is going to change what he does if I go

439
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:08,600
in, and then there's no point of entering.

440
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:10,360
So, and remember, we already talked

441
00:18:10,360 --> 00:18:11,680
about this model earlier on.

442
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:14,720
So this is often called the entry deterrence game.

443
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:20,320
So here's one where we got the options of in or out

444
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:21,480
for the entrant.

445
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:27,760
And then we have the options of accommodate or fight.

446
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:30,920
And then over here, the entrant makes nothing,

447
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:33,400
and the monopolist makes the monopoly profits.

448
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:36,800
And then over here, if the entrant goes in

449
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:39,160
and the incumbent decides to fight,

450
00:18:39,160 --> 00:18:43,280
then they both make minus A, minus A.

451
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:45,360
And then on the other hand, if the entrant goes in

452
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:48,320
and they accommodate, then maybe they each make,

453
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:53,200
say, one third of the monopoly profits each,

454
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:55,640
something like that.

455
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:57,920
And as you may recall, there is a problem

456
00:18:57,920 --> 00:19:02,040
with this story, which is that this equilibrium here,

457
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:05,480
this one over here, is not something perfect,

458
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:08,080
because the incumbent would not want to fight once

459
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:10,200
the entrant actually enters.

460
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:13,840
So that is the problem with this.

461
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:16,560
So this would be a case of the incumbent cutting off

462
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:18,880
his nose to spite his face, because once the entrant has

463
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:20,880
already entered, why not just go and settle

464
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:22,420
for a third of the monopoly profits?

465
00:19:22,420 --> 00:19:24,760
Why insist on getting revenge and saying,

466
00:19:24,760 --> 00:19:28,280
I'm going to hurt you for doing this?

467
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:32,280
Now, if you had a good teacher for industrialization

468
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:34,480
at some point who talked about predation,

469
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:36,560
they will have told you a bunch of other problems

470
00:19:36,560 --> 00:19:38,040
with standard predation stories.

471
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,480
And they might have even told you

472
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:42,000
about some actual economic history of predation,

473
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,000
like what actually happened with standard oil, things like that.

474
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:46,960
All right.

475
00:19:46,960 --> 00:19:49,480
So in this less formal literature that's out there,

476
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:52,040
but it's worth knowing that it exists,

477
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:54,400
people often emphasize that predation is especially

478
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,280
costly for the incumbents.

479
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:56,880
And why?

480
00:19:56,880 --> 00:19:59,480
Well, if you have 95% of the markets,

481
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:27,460
and then you can predatorize like a dog towards $1,000,


